 ### [S-NeRF Confidence Components](15152-s-nerf-confidence-components.md)
-S-NeRF Confidence Components is a selective confidence weighting strategy for differentiable volume rendering that adapts the supervision signal based on scene content, distinguishing between static background environments and dynamic foreground objects. It sets itself apart from standard NeRF regularization by explicitly decoupling the confidence metrics used for background (geometry and reprojection) versus foreground (RGB and SSIM), addressing the disparity in depth map quality between these regions.
+A selective confidence weighting strategy for dynamic street scenes, distinguishing between static background and dynamic foreground (vehicles). It applies geometry-aware confidence to backgrounds and RGB/SSIM-only metrics to vehicles, improving novel view synthesis quality. Choose this for autonomous driving datasets with unreliable depth for moving objects.
 
 ### [Depth Priors and Initialization](15188-depth-priors-and-initialization.md)
-#### Coarse-Fine Consistency Loss
-- [S1] A loss function that enforces consistency between depth estimates of the coarse and fine NeRF models to prevent ineffective hierarchical sampling and blur.
-- [S1] $L_{cfc} = \mathbb{1}_{\{m_f=1\}} \odot ||z_c - \hat{\nabla}(z_f)||^2 + \mathbb{1}_{\{m_c=1\}} \odot ||\hat{\nabla}(z_c) - z_f||^2$, where $z_c$ and $z_f$ are coarse and fine depths, and $m_c/m_f$ are reliability masks determined by reprojection.
+Not given in the sources.
 
-#### Indirect Depth Supervision (KL Loss)
-- [S2] A regularization technique that uses depth priors to constrain the NeRF weights by aligning the probability distribution of rendering weights with the depth prior, specifically using KL divergence.
-- [S2] $L_{KL} = \sum_{i}^{N_r} \sum_{t=t_n}^{t_f} D(r_i) - \epsilon \log w_k \exp(-\frac{(t - D(r_i))^2}{2\hat{\sigma}^2}) \Delta t_k$, where $w_k$ are rendering weights, $t_k$ are sampled points, $\Delta t_k$ are distances, $D(r_i)$ is ground truth depth, $\epsilon$ is a kernel parameter, and $\hat{\sigma}$ is a variance parameter.
-- [S2] $\epsilon(x)$: A kernel that integrates to one (a distribution) with a bounded domain parameterized by $\epsilon$.
-- [S2] The URF loss (Urban-NeRF) has no open-source implementation, so KL loss is selected as the representative for indirect supervision.
